---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/archaeology/foundational/what-archaeology-is">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the beginner lesson of Archaeology. You need no prior knowledge — no history
    course, no science background, no Latin. We start with your own rubbish bin and end
    at the scientific and ethical frontiers the discipline is navigating today. Every
    technical term is defined in plain words the moment it appears.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="find the faulty line in the Sutton Hoo story" widget="spot-error" config={arcSuttonHooSpot} />
  <Widget no="8" caption="the discipline's own stratigraphy" widget="timeline" config={arcDisciplineTimeline} />
  <p>By the end of this lesson you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>say what archaeology studies — and why dinosaurs are not on the list;</li>
    <li>explain why context is the science, and why excavation is destruction;</li>
    <li>outline the method: survey first, layers as a timeline, patterns as arguments;</li>
    <li>name the main dating clocks — radiocarbon, tree rings, style sequences — and their ranges;</li>
    <li>sketch how the discipline grew from gentlemanly collecting into a science, and name the kinds of archaeology practised today.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Take your time. Each paragraph carries one idea, and the whole method hangs on a
    single sobering rule that you will meet in a moment.
  </p>

  <h2>Start with your own rubbish</h2>
  <p>
    Imagine a future archaeologist digging up your household's bin from this week. What
    could she tell about you, without asking anyone anything? The packaging reveals what
    you ate and where it came from. The repair receipts reveal what you value. The ratio
    of wrappers to fresh-food scraps reveals how you shop. Even what is
    <em>absent</em> speaks: no nappies, no pet food, no beer bottles — each absence is a
    fact about your household. And she would know all this from things you never thought
    of as messages at all.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="read your own bin the way she would" widget="sort-buckets" config={arcBinBuckets} />
  <p>
    That is exactly the discipline. <strong>Archaeology</strong> is the study of the
    human past through its material remains — everything people left behind, from
    million-year-old stone tools to last century's landfills and sweet wrappers. It is
    the only field with direct access to the entire span of human existence. Writing
    covers roughly five thousand years; humans have been making and discarding things
    for over three million, and for most of that time the objects are all we have.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where historians read texts, archaeologists read the ground — and the ground records
    things no text ever thought to mention: what ordinary people ate, how they built,
    where they were buried, what they threw away, how their houses faced each other. It
    is, in that sense, the history of everyone, reconstructed from everyone's rubbish.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> archaeology reads ordinary discarded things the way you
    just read a bin — from remains to lives. The five steps below show how the
    discipline turns that reading into a science. Click through them now.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the method, from survival to inference" widget="equationStep" config={methodsCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> click through the five steps and watch one sobering
    rule generate the whole method — because the record is incomplete and excavation
    destroys the site, context must be captured before it is gone. Steps 3 and 4 are the
    practical consequences: map before digging, and read the stacked layers as a timeline.
    Step 5 is where data becomes argument — a single broken pot is an anecdote, a pattern
    of them across houses is an economy.
  </p>

  <h2>What archaeology studies</h2>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> first, the dinosaurs. Archaeologists study humans and
    human ancestors — fossil dinosaurs belong to palaeontology, a separate science.
    (The confusion is so universal that the discipline's outreach material opens with it.)
    Second, the treasure. The gold mask makes the museum poster, but the gold mask is
    evidence about metallurgy, trade and belief only because of <em>where it lay</em> — in
    what layer, with what objects, oriented how. That find-position, the provenance and
    association, is the science.
  </p>
  <p>
    The discipline runs on a sobering rule: <strong>excavation destroys the site</strong>.
    Digging is controlled, recorded destruction. You cannot re-dig a layer — once it is
    lifted, it is gone. So the record taken must outlast the site itself, which makes
    recording standards, archives and publication as much a part of the science as
    trowels. Everything in this lesson follows from that one fact.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="bet on the consequence of one rule" widget="predict" config={arcDestructionPredict} />
  <p>
    It follows first in the choice <em>not</em> to dig. Before any shovel,
    <strong>survey</strong> maps what is there. Fieldwalking collects material ploughed
    up by farmers. Geophysics images what is under the soil: resistivity surveys detect
    buried walls by their effect on soil moisture, and magnetometry detects ditches,
    hearths and kilns by their magnetism. Airborne <strong>lidar</strong> strips forest
    canopy in software, revealing earthworks no surveyor ever walked.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="pair each survey method with what it detects" widget="match" config={arcSurveyMatch} />
  <p>
    When excavation is justified, it proceeds by <strong>stratigraphy</strong>: layers
    accumulate oldest-first — a floor lies under the ashes that fell on it — and each
    layer is a unit of time containing a moment of a site's life. Recording is
    continuous: plans, sections, photographs, three-dimensional models. The
    <strong>Harris matrix</strong> formalises the stack of cut-and-fill relationships
    into one sequence — the site's history, in diagram form.
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally comes <strong>inference</strong>. Finds alone are anecdotes. Patterns are
    evidence: the same pottery style across forty sites is trade; animal bones sorted by
    butchery cut across a town is diet and economy; house floors rebuilt in the same
    footprints for two centuries is attachment to place. The artefact is a datum; the
    pattern is the argument.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="order the layers, bottom to top" widget="order" config={arcStratOrder} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> archaeology studies human remains of every kind, where
    context is the science and excavation is destruction — hence survey first, layers as
    a timeline, patterns as arguments. But the record itself has a bias that must be
    read honestly.
  </p>
  <p>
    The record is skewed, and honest archaeology begins by admitting it. Organic
    materials — wood, cloth, basketry — survive only in special conditions (waterlogged,
    frozen, very dry), so the surviving past over-represents stone, bone, pottery and
    fired clay. Wealth over-represents itself: burials of elites dominate the
    spectacular finds. Behaviour that left no objects — speech, law, song — survives
    only as inference from what did. The <strong>formation processes</strong> that
    created the record (discard, burial, erosion, later digging) are themselves objects
    of study.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="file each bias in the record" widget="sort-buckets" config={arcBiasBuckets} />
  <p>
    Dating is the discipline's structural spine, and it runs on a toolkit of
    complementary clocks. <em>Radiocarbon</em> covers organic material back about
    50,000 years, with a stated uncertainty — resolved by calibration curves built from
    tree rings, because atmospheric carbon-14 has not been constant.
    <em>Dendrochronology</em>, tree-ring dating, is exact to the year where long
    regional sequences exist: timbers in buildings and bogs match ring patterns against
    a master chronology. <em>Typology and seriation</em> order artefact styles by their
    frequencies across layers — the classical method, still indispensable where no
    datable organics survive. Beyond the radiocarbon range, other clocks take over:
    potassium–argon and its successors for early hominin sites, luminescence for
    sediments and when they were last exposed to light. Every good excavation reports
    several independent dates for the same layers, because the argument is strongest
    when disagreeing clocks are made to agree.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a radiocarbon date is an exact calendar date, like
  reading a year off a coin. It is a probability range, not a single year — the sample's
  carbon-14 content gives a spread of likely ages, and that raw spread must then be
  corrected through calibration curves, because the amount of carbon-14 in the
  atmosphere has wobbled through time. An honest date is reported as a range with its
  uncertainty, never as a bare year.</p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="guess the range of the carbon clock" widget="estimate" config={arcRadiocarbonEstimate} />

  <h2>The history of the discipline</h2>
  <p>
    Archaeology grew out of <strong>antiquarianism</strong>: the gentlemen collectors of
    the seventeenth to nineteenth centuries who mapped barrows, recorded stone circles
    and filled cabinets with curiosities. Their recording instincts were mixed, their
    methods destructive (a barrow tunnelled in a weekend), and their questions
    genealogical and romantic — but they preserved sites and started the idea that
    artefacts could be arranged in order.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first scientific breakthrough was chronological. Christian Jürgensen Thomsen's
    <em>Three Age System</em> (1836) organised the Copenhagen museum's collection into
    Stone, Bronze and Iron Ages from find associations — the first evidence-based
    framework for prehistory. Fieldwork standards rose through the century: General
    Pitt-Rivers in England insisted on total, planned recording of every find's position
    ("the value of relics depends on their associations"), and Flinders Petrie brought
    the same discipline to Egypt.
  </p>
  <p>
    The era's great drama — Heinrich Schliemann's trench through the mound of Troy in
    the 1870s — shows both faces at once. He found (by his identification) Homer's city
    and proved that Bronze Age civilisation lay beneath the classical world's feet. He
    also dug straight through the very strata that made his finds meaningful, and later
    archaeologists spent decades re-reading the damage. The profession's modern instinct
    — record everything, destroy nothing, publish or the excavation did not happen —
    was written partly in reaction to him.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> collecting became a science in three moves — order the
    finds (Thomsen), record the positions (Pitt-Rivers, Petrie), and date the organics
    (radiocarbon). The twentieth century added the theory.
  </p>
  <p>
    Culture-history mapped artefact styles onto peoples and migrations; Mortimer Wheeler
    and Kathleen Kenyon refined stratigraphic excavation into a rigorous, box-planned
    method. Then two revolutions remade the discipline. <strong>Radiocarbon dating</strong>
    (Willard Libby, 1949) allowed organic material to be dated directly from its
    carbon-14 content — freeing prehistory from guesswork and rapidly overturning
    chronologies built on cross-dating. And <strong>processual archaeology</strong>
    (Lewis Binford, from the 1960s) demanded that archaeology behave like science:
    state hypotheses about past systems — subsistence, exchange, settlement — and test
    them, treating the record as data about behaviour rather than a catalogue of styles.
    The <strong>post-processual</strong> response (Ian Hodder, from the 1980s) answered
    that past people were not atoms in a system but makers of meaning: symbols, agency,
    individual lives and the archaeologist's own position belong in the interpretation.
    The field still conducts that argument productively — usually by doing both.
  </p>

  <h2>Kinds of archaeology</h2>
  <p>
    The field's branches are defined by period, medium, method or purpose — and the
    spread is wider than most beginners expect. By period: <strong>prehistoric
    archaeology</strong> works where texts are absent, from the Palaeolithic to
    pre-contact America. <strong>Historical archaeology</strong> works alongside
    documents — colonial sites, plantations, whaling stations — often testing what the
    documents claim. <strong>Classical and biblical archaeology</strong> engage the
    oldest literate traditions.
  </p>
  <p>
    By medium: <strong>underwater (maritime) archaeology</strong> excavates shipwrecks
    and drowned landscapes, where airless mud preserves wood and cargo intact.
    <strong>Industrial archaeology</strong> records mills, railways and factories — the
    material history of the very recent past. <strong>Landscape archaeology</strong>
    studies whole regions — field systems, roads, boundaries — rather than single
    sites.
  </p>
  <p>
    By specialism: <strong>zooarchaeology</strong> reads animal bones (diet, herding,
    butchery); <strong>archaeobotany</strong> reads seeds and charcoal (crops, fuel,
    fields); <strong>bioarchaeology</strong> reads human remains (health, trauma,
    ancestry, migration); <strong>geoarchaeology</strong> reads soils and sediments
    (how sites formed). <strong>Experimental archaeology</strong> builds the boats,
    smelts the iron and knaps the tools to test what was possible.
    <strong>Ethnoarchaeology</strong> watches living societies make a record, to
    calibrate the inferences.
  </p>
  <p>
    By employment, the dominant kind is <strong>cultural resource management</strong>
    (CRM): the legally mandated survey, recording and mitigation of sites threatened by
    construction. Most professional archaeologists spend most of their careers there,
    which makes the discipline, in practice, a regulated conversation between
    development and heritage. <strong>Public and community archaeology</strong> extends
    the same logic to the people whose heritage is being dug.
  </p>
  <p>
    One famous excavation shows the whole method in a single story. In 1939, on a
    Suffolk estate, Basil Brown — a self-taught excavator working for landowner Edith
    Pretty — opened the ship-mound at Sutton Hoo and found, undisturbed, the burial
    chamber of an early Anglo-Saxon king: a 27-metre ship, helmet, gold buckle,
    Byzantine silver, Merovingian coins.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything that makes it science rather than spectacle came from the associations.
    The coins dated the burial to the early seventh century. The lack of a body raised
    the question — was it a <strong>cenotaph</strong>, a memorial over a grave lost to
    the river's acid soil, or a body that left nothing? The reach of the grave-goods —
    Frankish coins, Swedish helmet parallels, Mediterranean silver — turned a treasure
    into an argument about a kingdom, its trade and its politics at the edge of written
    history. The finds went to the British Museum under the law of treasure; the
    recording survived; the site is still being excavated. Modern CRM diggers work to
    exactly this standard, at roundabout sites.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> archaeology is treasure hunting with paperwork — that
  the aim is to extract valuable objects and the records are an afterthought. The Sutton
  Hoo story shows the truth: the objects' scientific value lived in their positions, and
  an unprovenanced find is evidence largely destroyed. This is why the field treats
  treasure hunting as the opposite of its method, and why context, not the object, is
  what gets preserved.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. The answers are right below.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why is a gold mask without its exact find-position scientifically worth so much less?</li>
    <li>A site may be threatened by a new road. Why does the archaeologist start with survey rather than digging?</li>
    <li>Wooden houses rarely survive. What does that tell you about reading the archaeological record?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Answers:</strong> (1) Because context <em>is</em> the evidence — the layer,
    the associations and the orientation carried the information about date, status and
    belief, and once the object is moved that evidence is gone forever. (2) Excavation
    destroys the site — it is controlled, unrepeatable destruction — so survey maps what
    is there first and excavation is used only where justified. (3) The record
    over-represents whatever survives — stone, bone, pottery — so the absence of wood is
    about preservation, not about what people built with.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Archaeology studies the human past through material remains — the only field with direct access to the entire span of human existence.</li>
    <li>Dinosaurs belong to palaeontology; archaeologists study humans and human ancestors.</li>
    <li>Context is the science: a find's provenance and associations carry most of its evidence, and once lost they are gone forever.</li>
    <li>Excavation is controlled destruction, so survey maps the site first and the record taken must outlast the site.</li>
    <li>Stratigraphy is the timeline: layers accumulate oldest-first, formalised in the Harris matrix.</li>
    <li>The record over-represents what survives — stone, bone, pottery and wealth — so honest reading starts by admitting the bias.</li>
    <li>Dating runs on complementary clocks: radiocarbon to about 50,000 years, tree rings exact to the year, style sequences where organics fail.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Later lessons go method by method — how a dig is planned, how finds are analysed in
    the laboratory, and how the frontier techniques previewed below (ancient DNA, isotope
    analysis, lidar) are rewriting particular prehistories. The deeper section follows the
    record itself as it enlarges, and the ethical questions that travel with it.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The scientific frontier keeps enlarging the record itself. <em>Ancient DNA</em>,
    extracted from bones since the 2010s, now reconstructs whole population movements —
    the peopling of Europe in successive waves, the Bantu expansions — sometimes
    overturning decades of artefact-based migration stories. <em>Isotope analysis</em>
    reads chemistry rather than genes: teeth lock in the isotopic signature of the water
    and food of childhood, so an individual buried far from home can be identified as a
    first-generation migrant; bone collagen reports on diet. <em>Lidar</em> delivered the
    2018 surprise over Guatemala's Petén: tens of thousands of previously unknown Maya
    structures — causeways, terraces, whole districts — under forest that has hidden them
    since European contact, forcing upward revision of Maya population by millions.
    <em>Proteomics</em> now reads residue from pots (what was actually cooked in them),
    and environmental archaeology reconstructs whole climates from the sediments around
    a site. The artefact catalogue has become a laboratory.
  </p>
  <p>
    The interpretive and ethical questions, meanwhile, stay stubbornly human.
    Repatriation is the defining one: indigenous communities reclaiming ancestors and
    sacred objects (in the US under NAGPRA, 1990; in parallel regimes worldwide),
    museums confronting how collections were formed, and archaeologists negotiating
    between scientific interest and descendant communities' rights — increasingly, co-
    managed research rather than excavation-then-consultation. The antiquities trade
    remains the discipline's enemy at the border: conflict-looted material still moves
    through market channels, and every unprovenanced object is a context destroyed.
    Open questions cluster where the science meets meaning: how to hear ordinary and
    marginal people in records dominated by architecture and wealth, how to
    distinguish collapse from transformation in the record of dead cities, and what a
    discipline built on digging should do when the best evidence says — as it often
    now does — to leave some sites in the ground for methods not yet invented.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Renfrew &amp; Bahn, <em>Archaeology: Theories, Methods and Practice</em>; Harris, <em>Principles of Archaeological Stratigraphy</em>. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
